1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,950
Hello everyone and welcome back to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:06,200
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:10,830
My name is Catherine and today we've got a great informative lesson for you.

4
00:00:11,400 --> 00:00:11,630
That's right.

5
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:14,610
We are going to talk about an emergency situation.

6
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:16,810
So what do you do when you have an emergency?

7
00:00:17,430 --> 00:00:18,510
You dial 911.

8
00:00:19,230 --> 00:00:19,480
Exactly.

9
00:00:19,480 --> 00:00:26,180
Alright, well maybe not in your country but today we're talking about emergencies in America and jobs.

10
00:00:26,180 --> 00:00:28,170
So people who can help you in an emergency.

11
00:00:28,560 --> 00:00:32,050
So before we get into those words and phrases, let's take a listen to today's dialogue.

12
00:00:33,600 --> 00:00:42,840
Alright class, now that we're all dressed up, let's see what professions you chose.

13
00:00:43,090 --> 00:00:46,270
I see a fireman, a police officer, a medic and a lifeguard.

14
00:00:46,830 --> 00:00:48,590
Can anyone tell me what these people have in common?

15
00:00:49,440 --> 00:00:51,070
They save people from bad things.

16
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:52,130
That's right.

17
00:00:52,840 --> 00:00:57,350
Now class, if something bad happened and you had to get help, do you know what phone number you would call?

18
00:00:57,710 --> 00:00:58,150
911.

19
00:00:59,350 --> 00:00:59,790
Yes!

20
00:01:00,570 --> 00:01:02,270
You would pick up the phone and dial 911.

21
00:01:03,110 --> 00:01:06,390
What are some emergency situations in which you would need to dial 911?

22
00:01:06,970 --> 00:01:15,000
If my grandpa had a heart attack, if there was an accident, if a robber broke into the house, if the fire alarm went off.

23
00:01:15,480 --> 00:01:18,320
I wouldn't call 911 if the fire alarm went off in my house.

24
00:01:18,680 --> 00:01:30,640
The only time that ever happens is when we're having spaghetti for supper and my mom burns the garlic bread as usual.

25
00:01:30,640 --> 00:01:31,130
Alright we're back.

26
00:01:31,640 --> 00:01:37,010
So now we have a couple of different jobs for people that are working specifically in emergency situations.

27
00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:39,890
So let's take a look at those now in Language Takeaway.

28
00:01:42,670 --> 00:01:43,530
Language Takeaway.

29
00:01:44,590 --> 00:01:49,230
Alright, so we have a teacher in today's dialogue who's talking to her students about jobs.

30
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:55,160
Now obviously these students are very young and she says another word that means jobs.

31
00:01:55,160 --> 00:01:55,950
What's the word she uses?

32
00:01:56,480 --> 00:01:56,990
Professions.

33
00:01:57,570 --> 00:01:58,170
Professions.

34
00:01:58,450 --> 00:01:58,970
That's the plural.

35
00:01:59,430 --> 00:02:00,810
So profession is a job.

36
00:02:01,590 --> 00:02:03,790
Let's say for example a lawyer is a profession.

37
00:02:04,880 --> 00:02:05,550
Or a doctor.

38
00:02:05,990 --> 00:02:07,150
A doctor, a lawyer.

39
00:02:07,850 --> 00:02:10,810
So what's your profession is another way to ask what's your job.

40
00:02:11,070 --> 00:02:18,440
Alright, so they're talking about professions and some of the kids dressed up as different people.

41
00:02:19,320 --> 00:02:21,280
So the first one was a fireman.

42
00:02:21,280 --> 00:02:21,880
A fireman.

43
00:02:22,280 --> 00:02:23,580
So you know the first word fire.

44
00:02:24,240 --> 00:02:27,320
A fireman is a person who puts out a fire.

45
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:30,560
That means he uses water to stop a fire.

46
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:30,720
Very good.

47
00:02:31,560 --> 00:02:33,920
So a fireman puts out fires.

48
00:02:33,920 --> 00:02:37,360
Now apart from a fireman we also have a police officer.

49
00:02:38,440 --> 00:02:39,360
A police officer.

50
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:43,120
So an officer is someone who works in an official capacity.

51
00:02:43,460 --> 00:02:43,940
So in a job.

52
00:02:44,580 --> 00:02:50,320
So we could say he is a police officer or a policeman or a policewoman.

53
00:02:50,320 --> 00:02:51,600
So three ways to say this.

54
00:02:51,960 --> 00:02:54,100
So officer is for both genders, right?

55
00:02:54,240 --> 00:02:56,760
A man or a woman could be a police officer.

56
00:02:56,760 --> 00:02:57,760
That's right.

57
00:02:57,760 --> 00:03:02,540
So you're going to hear this very often because policeman doesn't explain a woman police officer.

58
00:03:02,940 --> 00:03:04,780
So we use police officer to be general.

59
00:03:06,030 --> 00:03:09,450
So we have a fireman, a police officer, a medic.

60
00:03:09,990 --> 00:03:10,810
Now what is this medic?

61
00:03:11,440 --> 00:03:24,370
A medic is a kind of doctor, someone who will look at your health or help you if you've hurt yourself, who usually works on the road like driving to an accident or driving to a fire with police officers.

62
00:03:24,760 --> 00:03:28,090
So these people help police officers and firemen when there's an emergency.

63
00:03:28,570 --> 00:03:28,990
Okay.

64
00:03:29,760 --> 00:03:31,640
So a medic is not really a doctor.

65
00:03:31,640 --> 00:03:36,930
He's more of a specific person that can help you if you have maybe some cuts or bruises or will help you out.

66
00:03:37,200 --> 00:03:37,390
Right.

67
00:03:37,550 --> 00:03:43,950
So this person will stop your bleeding or will help take you to the hospital and they'll make sure that you're healthy when you get to the hospital.

68
00:03:44,450 --> 00:03:48,670
But they are not really like a doctor where you work in a hospital or a doctor's office.

69
00:03:49,030 --> 00:03:49,270
Okay.

70
00:03:49,910 --> 00:03:52,250
And our last profession is a lifeguard.

71
00:03:52,790 --> 00:03:53,990
Well think about these two words.

72
00:03:54,240 --> 00:03:56,360
We have life and we have guard.

73
00:03:56,360 --> 00:03:56,830
Guard is like protect.

74
00:03:57,360 --> 00:03:58,670
So a person that protects your life.

75
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:00,200
Hmm, interesting.

76
00:04:00,200 --> 00:04:04,680
Well this is someone who usually works at the beach or at a pool.

77
00:04:04,680 --> 00:04:13,680
They have a special chair and usually they have a cross on their bathing suit or on their towel and this means that they're going to help protect people who are at the beach.

78
00:04:14,080 --> 00:04:19,260
So if someone can't swim, the lifeguard will run or swim to get them and save them.

79
00:04:19,460 --> 00:04:20,480
Okay, very good.

80
00:04:20,680 --> 00:04:21,920
So you have a lifeguard.

81
00:04:22,420 --> 00:04:24,360
Now this is for men and women as well, right?

82
00:04:24,540 --> 00:04:24,980
A lifeguard.

83
00:04:25,220 --> 00:04:25,460
Exactly.

84
00:04:26,520 --> 00:04:27,520
Okay.

85
00:04:27,520 --> 00:04:27,900
So interesting professions.

86
00:04:28,520 --> 00:04:31,020
Why don't we listen to our dialogue again and we'll be back shortly.

87
