Ah, I see a fireman, a police officer, a medic and a lifeguard.

89
00:04:44,260 --> 00:04:46,020
Can anyone tell me what these people have in common?

90
00:04:46,880 --> 00:04:48,550
They save people from bad things.

91
00:04:49,240 --> 00:04:49,510
That's right.

92
00:04:50,240 --> 00:04:54,730
Now class, if something bad happened and you had to get help, do you know what phone number you would call?

93
00:04:55,720 --> 00:04:56,720
911.

94
00:04:56,770 --> 00:04:57,210
Yes.

95
00:04:57,970 --> 00:04:59,650
You would pick up the phone and dial 911.

96
00:05:00,490 --> 00:05:03,790
What are some emergency situations in which you would need to dial 911?

97
00:05:04,370 --> 00:05:06,050
If my grandpa had a heart attack.

98
00:05:06,610 --> 00:05:07,690
If there was an accident.

99
00:05:08,690 --> 00:05:10,190
If a robber broke into the house.

100
00:05:10,760 --> 00:05:12,400
If the fire alarm went off.

101
00:05:12,880 --> 00:05:15,740
I wouldn't call 911 if the fire alarm went off in my house.

102
00:05:16,060 --> 00:05:23,080
The only time that ever happens is when we're having spaghetti for supper and my mom burns the garlic bread as usual.

103
00:05:25,180 --> 00:05:27,640
Alright, we're back so now let's start with Fluency Builder.

104
00:05:29,080 --> 00:05:29,920
Fluency Builder.

105
00:05:31,440 --> 00:05:34,110
Our first item in Fluency Builder we've already talked about.

106
00:05:34,680 --> 00:05:40,280
The number in America to call when you have an emergency is 911.

107
00:05:40,280 --> 00:05:43,170
So we usually say, hey, dial 911.

108
00:05:43,730 --> 00:05:45,730
Okay, so that's a verb there, to dial.

109
00:05:46,280 --> 00:05:46,450
Exactly.

110
00:05:47,280 --> 00:05:50,430
To dial means to select the numbers for a phone call.

111
00:05:50,760 --> 00:05:51,650
To telephone someone.

112
00:05:52,010 --> 00:05:53,350
To telephone someone, to dial.

113
00:05:53,830 --> 00:05:57,470
So you can dial 911 or dial my office number.

114
00:05:58,360 --> 00:06:03,000
Or I tried to dial your home number but no one answered.

115
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:04,000
Okay.

116
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:07,430
Now you can call 911 for many different situations or emergency.

117
00:06:08,320 --> 00:06:11,350
One of the kids said, well, if my grandpa had a heart attack.

118
00:06:11,890 --> 00:06:14,250
Okay, so this is an example of an emergency.

119
00:06:14,730 --> 00:06:17,210
A heart attack is a medical problem.

120
00:06:17,830 --> 00:06:20,790
It's when your heart cannot receive oxygen.

121
00:06:21,410 --> 00:06:23,430
Okay, so your heart stops beating sometimes.

122
00:06:23,830 --> 00:06:24,230
That's right.

123
00:06:25,450 --> 00:06:25,890
Sorry.

124
00:06:26,250 --> 00:06:28,950
It's when your heart does not receive enough blood or oxygen.

125
00:06:28,950 --> 00:06:30,650
So it's when your heart stops beating.

126
00:06:30,990 --> 00:06:32,010
Okay, very good.

127
00:06:32,410 --> 00:06:33,810
So that's a heart attack.

128
00:06:34,230 --> 00:06:40,050
Now another possible emergency situation is if the fire alarm goes off.

129
00:06:40,430 --> 00:06:43,210
This often happens in office buildings and schools.

130
00:06:44,120 --> 00:06:49,760
So you hear a dee dee dee dee dee dee dee dee dee dee dee dee dee dee dee dee dee dee or some kind of strange noise.

131
00:06:49,760 --> 00:06:52,710
Well that's because there's maybe a fire or some smoke in your building.

132
00:06:53,720 --> 00:07:02,000
And so usually when this sound goes off, it automatically calls the police and the fire department.

133
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:03,000
Okay.

134
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:07,600
Now there's an interesting phrasal verb that is used along with this word fire alarm.

135
00:07:07,600 --> 00:07:11,800
We say the fire alarm goes off or the fire alarm went off.

136
00:07:12,260 --> 00:07:14,480
So that means it started to sound, to emit a sound.

137
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:19,160
So the verb here, as you said it's a phrasal verb, is to go off.

138
00:07:21,160 --> 00:07:22,910
We could say my alarm went off.

139
00:07:23,760 --> 00:07:27,990
Or every morning at 7 o'clock my alarm goes off.

140
00:07:28,250 --> 00:07:31,030
Okay, that means your alarm begins to make a sound.

141
00:07:31,330 --> 00:07:32,490
A very annoying sound.

142
00:07:32,840 --> 00:07:36,080
You know, it's the worst sound ever.

143
00:07:36,080 --> 00:07:37,090
But a lot of things can go off.

144
00:07:37,210 --> 00:07:39,910
We could talk about a bell or an alarm.

145
00:07:40,560 --> 00:07:40,970
Or fireworks.

146
00:07:41,560 --> 00:07:44,160
Or fireworks go off.

147
00:07:44,160 --> 00:07:50,110
And well, the last emergency that we were talking about is if a robber broke into the house.

148
00:07:50,840 --> 00:07:51,690
So if a robber breaks in.

149
00:07:52,560 --> 00:07:54,000
To break into.

150
00:07:54,020 --> 00:07:58,580
This means to enter, to go inside without permission or without a key.

151
00:07:59,020 --> 00:07:59,340
Illegally.

152
00:07:59,920 --> 00:08:00,920
Illegally.

153
00:08:00,920 --> 00:08:04,400
So you don't have to break something but usually you break a window or a door.

154
00:08:05,480 --> 00:08:09,520
But this means that someone comes into your house looking to steal something.

155
00:08:09,540 --> 00:08:11,100
So usually you have to use force.

156
00:08:11,290 --> 00:08:20,270
For example, if I forget my keys inside my car and I have to break into my own car, that means maybe I have to force the door open or maybe break the window.

157
00:08:20,610 --> 00:08:22,330
Yeah, it's not fun.

158
00:08:22,520 --> 00:08:25,040
I think it usually ends up being very expensive.

159
00:08:25,250 --> 00:08:25,990
Yes, it does.

160
00:08:26,270 --> 00:08:28,710
Alright, so those are all the phrases we have for today.

